From f75343040de7f0a15202e9bd0a6a9cbca4d10fa8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Johansen <john.johansen@canonical.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 09:27:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] apparmor: restrict unprivileged user namespace creation

Forward-port of Ubuntu's AppArmor userns-restriction SAUCE from their
7.0.0-30.30 distro kernel to vanilla 7.2. Extracted by diffing Ubuntu's
security/apparmor against vanilla v7.0 and taking only the userns hunks --
Ubuntu's AppArmor fork is ~5200 lines across 25 files, and almost none of
that is this feature.

Why this is needed at all: hyphaed builds from kernel.org, so it does not
get Ubuntu's SAUCE. Vanilla already has the mediation *mechanism* --
the userns_create LSM hook, apparmor_userns_create(),
aa_profile_ns_perm(),
AA_USERNS_CREATE and its audit path are all upstream, and the sibling knob
apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_unconfined was upstreamed too. What never
went upstream is the switch. So on a stock 7.2 build,
apparmor_userns_create() returns 0 for any unconfined task, which is
nearly
everything on a desktop, and unprivileged user namespaces are
unrestricted.

The visible symptom is that Ubuntu's own
/usr/lib/sysctl.d/10-apparmor.conf
tries to set kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1 on every boot
and fails with "No such file or directory". The invisible one is that the
machine is less confined than the -generic kernel beside it in GRUB,
which is not a tradeoff anyone chose.

What this adds:

  - CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR_RESTRICT_USERNS, default y, which seeds
    aa_unprivileged_userns_restricted.
  - kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns, plus _force and
    _complain.
    The first is mode 0644 with its own handler so an unprivileged
    reader can see whether the restriction is on -- container runtimes
    and browsers check it before attempting a userns -- while only a
    policy admin may write it. The other two stay 0600 as in Ubuntu.
  - ns_x_to_label(), which resolves a userns transition index to the label
    to
    switch to. Deliberately narrower than domain.c's x_to_label(): a
    userns
    transition can only be none or a table lookup, so anything else is
    rejected rather than guessed at.
  - aa_profile_ns_perm() now returns struct aa_label * instead of int, and
    apparmor_userns_create() installs the returned label on the new cred.

That last pair is the whole point, and it is why the smaller version of
this
patch was rejected. Denying the userns outright would have been about a
fifth
the code and would have broken things: Ubuntu's policy ships an
unprivileged_userns profile (and bwrap-userns-restrict) precisely so that
an
unprivileged task gets *confined and allowed* rather than refused. Chrome,
bwrap, flatpak and podman all depend on that. A deny-only port would look
like it worked, right up until a sandbox failed to start.

No upstream API drift needed handling. Everything ns_x_to_label() reaches
for
already exists in 7.2 -- x_table_lookup() is even already non-static with
a
declaration in include/domain.h -- and the only missing piece was the
ns.target member in the audit union, added here. Compiled clean with W=1.

NOT YET BOOTED, and that matters more than usual for this one. The whole
mechanism is a mediation path: if the transition is wrong, confinement
degrades quietly rather than failing loudly. Before trusting it:

  sysctl kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns   # must exist, = 1
  unshare -Ur true                          # confined, not denied
  aa-status | grep unprivileged_userns
  # then actually start chromium, a flatpak, and a podman container

Author is John Johansen, who wrote this in Ubuntu's tree; the forward-port
is
ours and adds no Signed-off-by, same carry convention as 0022 and 0023.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
